WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.090
China is offering a new
vision of the internet,

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.940
one which combines sweeping content curbs

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.770
with uncompromising data controls.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.260
The idea is called Cybersovereignty

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.830
and it's already spreading
around the world.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.023
This is your Bloomberg QuickTake
on the new Cyber Cold War.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:23.643
Welcome!

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.775
It's the new millennium,

00:00:27.792 --> 00:00:30.240
and a simpler time for the internet.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.920
Western tech pioneers proclaim
it's a borderless force

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.040
for transparency and individual freedom.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.750
But fast forward to now

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.290
and that's being challenged
like never before.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.450
A massive cyber attack affecting

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.140
nearly 50 million Facebook users.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.050
And it was my mistake, and I'm sorry.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.640
Even if you're not doing anything wrong,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.473
you're being watched.

00:00:50.473 --> 00:00:53.090
You want to win a war,
you need weapons for that.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.260
And we could build them.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.910
There's been a global backlash against

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.720
Facebook, Google, WhatsApp.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.710
This backlash coincides with
the rise of the Chinese model.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.590
Today you have governments
from Russia to Southeast Asia

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.080
saying, hang on, we get to
control content on the internet

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.030
and at the same time
there is not an impediment

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.870
to stunning economic growth.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.670
It's an ideological coup and a rejection

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.390
of the American internet
model, which promised to spur

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.190
innovation and freedom.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.580
Now China is offering a different vision:

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.115
both internet control and tech
innovation, and it has fans.

00:01:32.115 --> 00:01:35.650
Today, there would be a
growing number of people

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.000
who would argue that controlling
the flow of information

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.523
across the internet does not
actually impede innovation.

00:01:45.447 --> 00:01:47.320
The crux of the Chinese internet model

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.540
is based around the nation state.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.990
Setting your own rules
for your own citizens

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.510
that can't be circumvented
by the internet.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.350
So very simply, they want to control

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:00.040
what sort of content is
hosted on the internet

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.880
that's available to Chinese users

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.050
and they want absolute
control over that content.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.510
So if they decide, for
instance, that they don't want

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.713
any references to the
Tiananmen Massacre from 1989,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.813
then they will scrub that out
of every website and every app

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.690
within the country that
Chinese or consumers can see.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.780
And this controlled, moderated
version of the internet

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.970
is spreading, especially
across Southeast Asia.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.890
Vietnam's controversial version

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.910
of the Chinese internet model
went into effect in 2019.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.160
It demands the data of Vietnamese users

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.180
is kept in the country.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.210
Indonesia, Southeast
Asia's largest economy,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.080
already requires data
to be stored locally.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.610
The Philippines has stepped up

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.320
what critics call a media crackdown

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.700
and one of the latest to
buy into the rationale

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.900
is Thailand, which passed
a cyber security bill

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.060
modeled on China's.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.050
Southeast Asia is the testing
ground for Chinese ambitions.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.000
The region is home to more
than half a billion people

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.610
whose internet economy
is expected to triple

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.030
to 240 billion dollars by 2025.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.560
I think a lot of Chinese companies,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.710
and potentially Beijing itself,

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:19.330
see Southeast Asia as the first step in

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.230
expanding their influence globally

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:25.980
but that's when the digital
Cold War kind of differs from

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.250
the Cold War we're familiar with.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.090
In this case, I think
countries are adopting

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.380
the Chinese style model
without necessarily subscribing

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.070
to Beijing's style of government
and/or Beijing's agenda.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.220
China's version of the
internet is appealing

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.940
to other nations who want to control

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.570
what their populations see and hear,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.280
but it doesn't mean
China's calling the shots.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.080
Instead it could create an
unprecedented bifurcation

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.750
of the internet, effectively
ending our notion

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.740
of a truly worldwide web,

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.470
meaning what information
you can easily access

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.130
would depend on where you are

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.580
and what that government
decides you should know.

